From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 18:40:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] The alpha test's two uses are not one question

Patch 0014 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

Patch 0013's rungs produced a clean result and a clean regression at once. The user, 2026-09-30:
the alpha tested rung "seems to fix tree issue but regress on shadows from sun covering navi",
while the rung without it "fixes the navi sun shadow conflict but shows the tringles".

The two rungs differ in one thing, so the cause is that one thing, and the answer is that the
coverage this test returns is used for two different questions.

The any hit uses it as a yes or no: nothing of the surface falls in this pixel, so the ray passes
through. That is what removes the triangles and it is wanted. The closest hit writes the same
number into the payload as a WEIGHT, and the direct pass does lerp(1, factor, coverage), so a
partial answer pulls the factor toward one, which is no shadow and no light together. On a leafy
cliff, whose texture alpha is a gradient rather than a clean cut, the five tap estimate lands
between zero and one across much of the surface: harmless as a yes or no, ruinous as a weight.
That is a fairy's glow going missing on a sun shadowed wall.

On these rungs the two uses are separated. The any hit keeps its rejection, and a surface that
survives it reports itself whole and takes its full share of light. Rung 20 keeps the shadow off;
rung 21 puts it back, which with the rejection in place is a leaf casting a leaf.

Only draws in the translucent z mode that never write depth are affected, which is the class the
cut-out rules reclassify. Everything else keeps the coverage it had, including every cut-out the
game declares properly, so the 2026-09-28 coverage work is untouched.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -1270,6 +1270,29 @@ void SurfaceClosestHit(inout SurfacePayload payload, in BuiltInTriangleIntersect
     // here, on the hit that won, rather than kept from an any hit that may have run for another
     // candidate last.
     payload.coverage = alphaCoverage(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex(), attributes.barycentrics, true);
+
+    // THE TEST'S TWO USES ARE NOT THE SAME QUESTION (patch 0014, the user 2026-09-30: alpha tested
+    // "seems to fix tree issue but regress on shadows from sun covering navi", while the rung
+    // without it "fixes the navi sun shadow conflict but shows the tringles").
+    //
+    // The any hit uses this as a YES or NO: nothing of the surface here, so the ray goes on. That
+    // is what kills the triangles and it is wanted. The line above uses the SAME number as a
+    // WEIGHT, and the direct pass does lerp(1, factor, coverage), so a partial answer pulls the
+    // factor toward one, which is no shadow and no light at once. On a leafy cliff, whose alpha is
+    // a gradient rather than a clean cut, the five tap estimate lands between zero and one over
+    // much of the surface: harmless as a yes or no, ruinous as a weight, and that is the fairy's
+    // glow going missing on a sun shadowed wall.
+    //
+    // So on this rung the two uses are separated. The any hit keeps its rejection; the surface
+    // that survives it reports itself whole and takes its full share of light.
+    if (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x >= 20.0f) {
+        const RenderIndices ri = instanceRenderIndices[InstanceID()];
+        const RenderParams rp = DynamicRenderParams[ri.instanceIndex];
+        const OtherMode om = { rp.omL, rp.omH };
+        if ((om.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) && !om.zUpd() && (payload.coverage > 0.0f)) {
+            payload.coverage = 1.0f;
+        }
+    }
 }
 
 // THE ALPHA TEST AT THE HIT (patch 0008). The game draws its tree lines, cliff tops, vines,
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1879,6 +1879,13 @@ namespace RT64 {
                             case 17: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; castsNothing = true; break;
                             case 18: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
                             case 19: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
+                            // 20 and 21 are rung 17 and rung 18 with the alpha test's two uses
+                            // separated (patch 0014): the any hit still rejects a see-through
+                            // texel, but a surface that survives the rejection reports itself
+                            // whole, so it takes its full share of light instead of being faded
+                            // out by a partial coverage. 20 keeps the shadow off, 21 puts it back.
+                            case 20: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; castsNothing = true; break;
+                            case 21: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
                             default: solidDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER); break;
                         }
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
